Chapter: ScopeAllow

  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 The positive half of the scope check. This opening grants itself
 FileSystem.Read under "/config/" and reads only inside it, so the
 scope permits the access and the program compiles.

 The second read is the narrowing case: "/config/sub/deep.toml" sits
 under the granted prefix, and a scope that admits its own subtree is
 the whole point -- narrowing is always allowed.

 An unscoped grant covers everything, which is why every program that
 names no scope is untouched by this check.

 What this test asserts at runtime is that both files are ABSENT, and it
 used to assert the opposite. file-exists was a builtin whose whole body
 was `li rd, 1`, so it answered True for every path, and this test's
 expected output recorded that answer as though it were a fact. The green
 battery was pinning the bug: whoever fixed file-exists would have found
 this test red and been invited to "fix" the fix. No disk is attached to
 this VM and nothing is under /config/, so absent is the truth. The scope
 check is a compile-time judgement about what this program is permitted
 to ask, and it is proved by the program compiling at all -- not by what
 the answer turns out to be.

 Device.Block is declared because asking whether a file exists means
 asking the disk, and the effect row is where a program says what it
 touches.

Section: Body
  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read "/config/", Device.Block] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (if file-exists "/config/app.toml" then "top in scope" else "top absent")
    print-line-uni (if file-exists "/config/sub/deep.toml" then "deep in scope" else "deep absent")
  end
